Instead of saving the first value, this uses neighboring swaps. Swapping index `0` with `1`, then `1` with `2`, and so on moves the original first value to the end while shifting the rest left.

Dry run for `[3, 6, 8, 10]`:
`[6, 3, 8, 10]`, then `[6, 8, 3, 10]`, and finally `[6, 8, 10, 3]`.

An empty array or a one-item array already has the required order.

```javascript
class Solution {
    rotateArrayByOne(nums) {
        const n = nums.length;
        if (n <= 1) {
            return;
        }

        for (let i = 0; i < n - 1; i++) {
            const temp = nums[i];
            nums[i] = nums[i + 1];
            nums[i + 1] = temp;
        }
    }
}
```

Time complexity: `O(n)`  
Space complexity: `O(1)`

This version keeps the original list unchanged until the new order has been prepared. It makes a copy, then writes the value at index `i` into index `i - 1`, with the first value wrapping to the final position.

The extra list is not needed for this small task, but it makes the source values safe while the destination list is being updated.

```csharp
using System.Collections.Generic;

public class Solution
{
    public void rotateArrayByOne(List<int> nums)
    {
        if (nums == null || nums.Count <= 1)
        {
            return;
        }

        List<int> original = new List<int>(nums);
        int n = original.Count;

        for (int i = 1; i < n; i++)
        {
            nums[i - 1] = original[i];
        }

        nums[n - 1] = original[0];
    }
}
```

Time complexity: `O(n)`  
Space complexity: `O(n)`

A left rotation by one can also be seen as two blocks: the array without its first value, followed by that first value. Reversing the first block, reversing the last block, and then reversing everything gives the correct order without another array.

For `[1, 2, 3, 4, 5]`, the steps are `[4, 3, 2, 1, 5]`, then `[4, 3, 2, 1, 5]` after reversing the one-item block, and finally `[5, 1, 2, 3, 4]`. The code changes `nums` directly.

```java
class Solution {
    public void rotateArrayByOne(int[] nums) {
        int n = nums.length;
        if (n <= 1) {
            return;
        }

        reverse(nums, 0, n - 2);
        reverse(nums, n - 1, n - 1);
        reverse(nums, 0, n - 1);
    }

    private void reverse(int[] nums, int left, int right) {
        while (left < right) {
            int temp = nums[left];
            nums[left] = nums[right];
            nums[right] = temp;
            left++;
            right--;
        }
    }
}
```

The single-element case and an empty array need no work.  
Time complexity: `O(n)`  
Space complexity: `O(1)`

The first value belongs at the end after a left rotation. I save it, move every other value one place to the left, and then put the saved value in the last position.

For example, `[1, 2, 3, 4]` becomes `[2, 3, 4, 1]`. The array is changed in place.

```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    void rotateArrayByOne(vector<int>& nums) {
        int n = nums.size();
        if (n <= 1) {
            return;
        }

        int first = nums[0];
        for (int i = 1; i < n; i++) {
            nums[i - 1] = nums[i];
        }
        nums[n - 1] = first;
    }
};
```

Time complexity: `O(n)`  
Space complexity: `O(1)`

